        "surveyDate": "2019-09-09",
        "narrative": "<p>Brule Lake lies within the Lake Superior North watershed, and is the headwaters for the Temperance and the Brule Rivers.  The lake is entirely inside the Boundary Waters Canoe Area Wilderness, and no motorized use is allowed anywhere on its waters.  Primary and secondary management species in Brule Lake are walleye and northern pike.  Long range goals for walleye are to maintain a minimum gill net catch of 3.0 fish/set, with some fish over 25 inches present.  For northern pike long range goals are a minimum gill net catch of 0.5 fish/set, with some fish over 25 inches present.  The 2019 survey was the second of two scheduled in the 2008 lake management plan to update information on water quality, habitat, and the fish community in the lake, and to monitor the lake for any introduction of exotic species.  The first of those surveys was completed in 2009.  Based on these surveys, the management plan for this lake will be revised in December 2020; comments on the lake's management are welcomed at any time before that date. <br> \n <br> \nWalleye were fairly abundant in 2019, and their average size was better than average for this lake historically.  The gill net catch in 2019 was within the normal range for the lake class, and was about average for Brule Lake.  The catch met the long range goal for walleye in terms of numbers of fish, but failed to meet the size goal (no fish over 25 inches were taken).  Similar results had been seen in the last (2009) survey.  Twelve year classes, all produced naturally, contributed to the 2019 catch, including a strong year class in 2012 and moderately strong year classes in 2016 and 2017.  The 2005 year class, reported in the 2009 survey as exceptionally strong, continued to contribute to the walleye population in 2019.  Growth of walleye collected in 2019 had been somewhat below average; fish reached a mean length of 17.7 inches at the end of their seventh year of growth, compared to an area mean of 19.7 inches.   <br> \n <br> \nSlow growth among walleye may have been related to low yellow perch abundance.  Yellow perch catches have typically been low in this lake, and the 2019 catch was no exception, falling below the normal range for this type of lake.  Catches of zero yellow perch have been common in this lake; none were taken in gill nets in the four assessments done between 1979 and 1996. Very few small yellow perch were taken in quarter-inch trap nets in 2019 - a further indication that the yellow perch forage base was limited.   Very few minnows or other small fish species were taken in quarter-inch trap nets in 2009 and 2019, suggesting that diversity of the Brule Lake nearshore fish community may have been low, even for a lake in this area, and that nearshore forage fish were scarce. <br> \n <br> \nNorthern pike were low in numbers in 2019, and all of the fish taken were young and small.  The 2019 northern pike gill net catch met the current long range goal, in terms of numbers of fish, but failed to meet the size goal (no fish over 25 inches).  The catch was similar to most seen historically in this lake, although well below catches seen in 1996 and 2009.  Only two year classes contributed to the 2019 catch, with no fish older than three years taken.  Growth of young northern pike had been about average for this area; they reached a mean length of 14.9 inches at lthe end of their second year, compared to an area mean of 15.3 inches.  Brule Lake provides the cool water and the cisco forage base needed to produce large northern pike, but mean weights for the species have typically been low compared to other area lakes with similar conditions.  Given the lake's large size, and limited angler use (due to motor restrictions), it is unlikely that harvest has limited the lake's ability to produce larger fish.   <br> \n <br> \nThe 2019 survey yielded one of the higher smallmouth bass catches seen in this lake historically, although the catch remained about average for a lake of this type.  Smallmouth bass taken in 2019 tended to be large, with a mean weight that was above the normal range for the lake class.  <br> \n <br> \nThe cisco gill net catch in 2019 was about average for the lake, historically, and appeared to show no signs of decline.  Based on the length frequency distribution, it appeared that at least two, and possibly three, strong year classes contributed to the 2019 catch.  Past surveys have shown that older walleye in this lake feed heavily on cisco, while younger fish rely more on perch and invertebrates. <br> \n <br> \nNo new fish species were found in Brule Lake in 2019.  The lake is not known to be infested with any exotic species.</p>"
